---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CompressImageToTarget from '@/components/tools/image-edit/CompressImageToTarget.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="compress-png-to-100kb">
  <CompressImageToTarget client:load targetKb={100} fixedFormat="png" inputLabel="Drop the PNG files you need at 100 KB" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>PNG has no quality dial, and that changes everything</h2>
    <p>
      In JPEG, one number trades fidelity for bytes. PNG cannot do that: it is lossless, so each pixel
      comes back exactly as it went in, and there is no setting to lower. Only two things can change:
      the number of pixels and the number of distinct colours. For this reason, with PNG output this
      tool does a binary search of the scale factor, not a quality value, and reports the final
      dimensions.
    </p>

    <h2>Is PNG the right format for your image?</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Content</th><th>Keep as PNG?</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Screenshot of an interface</td><td>Yes</td><td>Sharp text and flat panels compress losslessly to very little; JPEG would add rings around each letter</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Logo, icon, line art</td><td>Yes</td><td>Few colours, hard edges: the ideal case for PNG, and alpha is kept</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Chart or diagram</td><td>Yes</td><td>Large flat fills, sharp lines</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Photograph saved as PNG</td><td>No</td><td>Often ten times larger than the same picture as a JPEG with no visible difference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Photo with transparency</td><td>Use WebP</td><td>Keeps the alpha channel but compresses the photo lossily</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      If your PNG is a photograph, change the output format to JPG or WebP in the options. This one
      change usually takes a 3 MB file under 100 KB with no resizing.
    </p>

    <h2>Why PNG files get so big</h2>
    <p>
      PNG predicts each pixel from its neighbours and compresses the differences with Deflate. This
      works very well on flat colour and repeated patterns, and almost not at all on photographic
      noise, where each pixel differs slightly from its neighbours. A lossless codec can hardly
      compress a camera photo, which is the reason lossy formats exist.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting a real PNG under 100 KB</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Reduce the dimensions. The search does this automatically; half the area gives about half the bytes. For a screenshot, export at 1× instead of Retina 2×; that is often enough.</li>
      <li>Crop the chrome. Screenshots often include frame that you do not need. Removing it saves bytes and makes the image clearer.</li>
      <li>Use fewer colours. Gradients, drop shadows and blur effects add thousands of unique colours to regions that would be flat, and PNG must encode each transition.</li>
      <li>Check for a hidden photo. A screenshot with a photographic hero image is a photo to the encoder.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Transparency</h2>
    <p>
      PNG and WebP both have an alpha channel; JPEG does not. If your image has transparent regions and
      you change the output to JPG, those regions become solid, which is almost never correct for a
      logo. WebP has alpha support plus lossy compression, so a transparent product shot can go under
      100 KB and stay sharp.
    </p>

    <h2>What the search shows you</h2>
    <p>
      There is no quality axis, so the attempt table lists scale percentages: 100%, then 55%, then 77%,
      until it finds the largest size that fits. The last row gives the final dimensions. If they are
      much smaller than you need, change the format; do not continue to shrink.
    </p>

    <h2>Local, lossless and unlimited</h2>
    <p>
      Resizing uses a Lanczos3 filter, and a WebAssembly PNG encoder in your browser does all encoding.
      Nothing is uploaded, there is no per-file limit, and you can try as often as you like. This
      matters here, because the right balance between dimensions and format usually takes two or three
      attempts.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/compress-jpeg-to-100kb">Compress a JPEG to 100 KB</a> ·
      <a href="/png-to-jpg">PNG to JPG</a> ·
      <a href="/png-to-webp">PNG to WebP</a> ·
      <a href="/reduce-image-size-in-kb">any target you type</a>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
